he number of holes in the graph, $b$ be the number of vertical asympotes, $c$ be the number of horizontal asymptotes, and $d$ be the number of oblique asymptotes. Find $a+2b+3c+4d$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"95c905b59d62f43d9d3fe7dba9df18ea5eefbbfa6be9a253d7b85e1009e8fecc"},"seconds":43.83229684829712,"seed":327654407750738849,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem involving the rational function \\(\\frac{x^3 - x^2 + x}{6x^2 - 9x}\\), we need to analyze the function for holes, vertical asymptotes, horizontal asymptotes, and oblique asymptotes.\n\nFirst, simplify the function by factoring the numerator and the denominator.\n\nThe numerator is \\(x^3 - x^2 + x\\):\n\\[x^3 - x^2 + x = x(x^2 - x + 1)\\]\n\nThe denominator is \\(6x^2 - 9x\\):\n\\[6x^2 - 9x = 3x(2x - 3)\\]\n\nThus, the function can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n\\frac{x(x^2 - x + 1)}{3x(2x - 3)}\n\\]\n\nWe can cancel the common factor \\(x\\) from the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{x(x^2 - x + 1)}{3x(2x - 3)} = \\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)}\n\\]\n\nThis simplification shows that there is a hole at \\(x = 0\\). Therefore, \\(a = 1\\).\n\nNext, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator of the simplified function equal to zero:\n\\[\n3(2x - 3) = 0 \\implies 2x - 3 = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThere is one vertical asymptote at \\(x = \\frac{3}{2}\\). Therefore, \\(b = 1\\).\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptotes, we compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator in the simplified function \\(\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{3(2x - 3)}\\):\n- The degree of the numerator is 2.\n- The degree of the denominator is 1.\n\nSince the degree of the numerator is greater than the degree of the denominator, there is no horizontal asymptote. Therefore, \\(c = 0\\).\n\nTo find the oblique asymptotes, we perform polynomial long division of the numerator by the denominator in the simplified function \\(\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{6x - 9}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 - x + 1}{6x - 9} = \\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{13/4}{6x - 9}\n\\]\n\nThe quotient of the division, \\(\\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{1}{4}\\), gives us the oblique asymptote:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{1}{6}x + \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus, there is one oblique asymptote. Therefore, \\(d = 1\\).\n\nFinally, we compute \\(a + 2b + 3c + 4d\\):\n\\[\na + 2b + 3c + 4d = 1 + 2(1) + 3(0) + 4(1) = 1 + 2 + 0 + 4 = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
